Why: ToolRegistry::wrap_host_authorized lets an embedding app that replaces a tool with its own implementation authorize the calls the driver admits only in unrestricted mode, with its own check, so unrestricted mode can stay off.

The driver refuses page's script actions unless the whole runtime runs in
unrestricted mode, because its own page implementation is unbounded. A host
that swaps in its own page tool, with its own target rules, had no way to
admit those calls short of turning on unrestricted mode for every tool.
wrap_host_authorized(name, authorize, wrap) is wrap() plus a host check that
takes the mode's place for that one tool; every other check still applies,
and the driver's own implementation is dropped. Upstreamable as is.

